Halve PNG encode time by fixing the effort-to-level mapping

OxiPNG's levels do not trade time for size monotonically. Measured on a
2400x1600 PNG:

  level 1 -> 2.05 MB in 1.4s
  level 2 -> 1.77 MB in 3.4s
  level 3 -> 1.80 MB in 8.5s

Level 3 costs 2.5x level 2 and comes out larger, and the default effort mapped
straight onto it. Effort now maps through a measured lookup table instead of
arithmetic, so the default lands on 2: PNG at default settings goes from 8.5s
to ~3.5s and gets slightly smaller at the same time.

Also send 16-bit PNGs directly to OxiPNG rather than through the canvas. The
normal path reads pixels back via getImageData, which is 8 bits per channel and
silently halves the precision of a 16-bit source -- not something this app
should do while calling the format lossless. Verified against a hand-built
16-bit PNG: the direct path keeps bit depth 16, the canvas path truncates to 8.

Kept deliberately narrow. Routing every PNG through OxiPNG's own decoder was
the obvious generalisation and measured slower (3.9s vs 3.4s), because it
decodes in wasm instead of reusing the browser's native decoder, for an
identical result. Resize still forces the canvas path, since resizing needs one.

